Graphics Environment Manager

Historical origin
-----------------
Graphics Environment Manager did not originate with this repository. The
original Graphics Environment Manager was developed by Digital Research with
Lee Jay Lorenzen and first released on February 28, 1985. Gary Kildall oversaw
its creation at Digital Research. Dorothy McEwen Kildall co-founded Digital
Research and managed the company's marketing and daily operations.

This separate modern application is not the original Digital Research desktop
and contains no original Digital Research source code. Historical acknowledgment
does not imply affiliation with or endorsement by Digital Research or the
Kildall family.

References:
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/GEM_(desktop_environment)
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gary_Kildall
- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dorothy_McEwen_Kildall

Canonical modern master and standalone distribution
---------------------------------------------------
The canonical modern master is the gem-lab distribution in Ablation:
https://github.com/Ablation-Tool/ablation

This standalone repository contains that master's actual native workbench,
including its GTK and Tk interfaces, METRIX terminal components, charts,
Spatial IR, VDI support, preferences, session protocol, installers, and stress
suites. It is not an independently redesigned interface. The standalone
packaging, version metadata, documentation, and supplied PNG/ICO/ICNS window
and desktop-installer icons are retained, and optional local-model prediction
and advisory capabilities are added.

Accessible-volume research, the equation, experiments, and paper are solely the
work of Nicholas Michael Kloster. The equation was created on March 6, 2026.
Canonical research and measurement harness:
https://github.com/Ablation-Tool/next-token-predictability

The prediction integration adapts that harness for live Lab selections. The
advisory and equation-tournament layers are experimental deterministic
heuristics and sensitivity instruments, not correctness, safety, authorization,
or autonomous-execution systems.

The original Ablation project is distributed under GPL-3.0-or-later. This
standalone distribution retains that license; see LICENSE.

Upstream copyright and component notices for Ubuntu Update Manager, GNOME
Baobab, Vanilla Framework icons, terminal palettes, and related materials are
preserved in UPSTREAM-NOTICE and licenses/.

The terminal emulator uses pyte (MIT) and tkinterdnd2 (MIT), plus pywinpty
(MIT) on native Windows. Those packages retain their own licenses.

The supplied application icon is stored as gem/assets/gem2red.png with native
ICO and ICNS variants. The original /media path is not a runtime dependency.

Landmark example attribution
----------------------------
The landmark-alignment worked example uses the caption "Aligning two shapes by
minimizing the distance of corresponding landmark points" from "Linear Algebra?"
by Daniel Cohen-Or, Olga Sorkine-Hornung, and Chen Greif. Approximate landmark
coordinates were extracted from a user-supplied screenshot for mathematical
analysis. The original figure and screenshot are not redistributed in this
repository.

Multiscale visual attention
---------------------------
`gem.saliency_cognition` is an independent NumPy implementation of the feature
and attention equations published by Laurent Itti, Christof Koch, and Ernst
Niebur in "A Model of Saliency-Based Visual Attention for Rapid Scene Analysis"
(IEEE PAMI 20(11), 1998). The iLab Neuromorphic Vision Toolkit and its published
source snapshot were used as references; no iLab source file is bundled here.

References:
- https://ilab.usc.edu/publications/doc/Itti_etal98pami.pdf
- https://ilab.usc.edu/bu/theory/index.html
- https://ilab.usc.edu/toolkit/downloads.shtml

The toolkit identifies GPL-2.0-or-later and a historical patent notice. This
project retains GPL-3.0-or-later. These acknowledgments do not assert patent
clearance, affiliation, biological validation, or endorsement by USC/iLab.

A public Vivotek sample image was used only for local input testing. The sample
is not redistributed in this repository, and no Vivotek firmware or runtime is
included. Source: https://ftpmirror.your.org/pub/misc/ftp.vivotek.com/snapshotd.jpg

Soroban and ecological working memory
-------------------------------------
The soroban representation was informed by user-supplied notes on bead state,
place value, carries, borrows, and working memory. The implementation is original
and operates on explicit bead configurations with reversible checkpoints. No
example C program is copied, and no physical abacus or other hardware is actuated.

Ecological and rehearsal references include Varga and Heck on respiration
(https://doi.org/10.1101/174276), Adamatzky et al. on fungal electrical activity
(https://doi.org/10.1101/2022.04.03.486900), Wang et al. on prediction and
feedback (https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.06.10.658968), and Xu et al. on prosodic
recitation training (https://doi.org/10.1038/s42003-026-10664-4). These sources
inform experiment structure and provenance. They do not validate a biological
model, a mental-abacus mechanism, or a claim of consciousness.
